  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 304 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThis Evening through Sunday night...\n\nWidespread low clouds will continue across the area this afternoon \ninto this evening, along with a few sprinkles or light showers \nduring the daylight hours, as a warm front approaches and a subtle \nshortwave passes through the area. Some clearing may occur this \nevening before ample low level moisture in the presence of both a \nstout inversion aloft and the aforementioned boundary, along with \nlight to calm to surface winds, promotes the redevelopment of low \nclouds and fog, which may become dense in spots late tonight.\n\nCloud cover and fog may be slow to mix out tomorrow underneath the \naforementioned inversion, courtesy of a stout elevated mixed layer, \nbut we should see breaks of sunshine by midday as the boundary moves \nfurther into the region and PBL mixing increases. The inversion will \nkeep a strong lid on an otherwise fairly strongly unstable airmass \nthrough the day, with chances for thunderstorms increasing during \nthe evening and into the overnight hours Sunday night.\n\nPlentiful instability and shear will be present, though significant \nuncertainty exists regarding exactly where upstream thunderstorm \nactivity will initiate, how exactly these storms will organize, and \nthe orientation of the instability gradient that will largely drive \ntheir motion. CAMs are struggling to converge on a scenario, which \nis not surprising given the pattern and time of year. A wide \nenvelope of scenarios exists for tomorrow night into early Monday, \nfrom little to no convection, to a potent QLCS pushing into the area \nwith damaging wind potential.\n\nTemperatures will depend heavily upon clearing tomorrow and \nthunderstorm activity tomorrow night, so uncertainty is higher than \nwould prefer, but assuming we see good sun tomorrow afternoon, highs \nwill likely rise well into the 80s, with overnight lows limited by \ndewpoints largely to the low 70s.\n\nMonday and Monday night...\n\nChances for convection will be higher late Monday into Monday night, \nas somewhat more strongly forced scenario is likely with both a \nsurface cold front and shortwave trough supporting thunderstorm \ndevelopment. However, uncertainty still remains high in the exact \ntiming and details, given the influence that potential prior \nconvection will have on subsequent episodes, and the heavy \ndependence upon the thermodynamic parameter space for determining \nexactly how and where these MCSs will impact. Suffice to say, \nplentiful instability and shear will likely be present across the \nregion, and any storms that move into the area will possess \npotential for damaging winds. Additionally, precipitable water \nvalues will approach 2 inches at times, which along with deep and \nefficient warm rain processes may set the stage for heavy rainfall \nand localized flash flooding.\n\nTemperatures are again uncertain owing primarily to convective \npotential, but highs well into the low to mid 90s are definitely on \nthe table Monday, which could drive afternoon heat index values well \ninto the triple digits in spots.\n\nTuesday onward...\n\nCold frontal passage Monday night into early Tuesday will bring \ntemperatures back down closer to seasonal normals from mid week \nonward, with a largely dry forecast until very late in the week as \nsurface high pressure dominates the area. As the ridge aloft \nretrogrades slightly, upper level disturbances late in the week may \npromote rain chances, though significant disagreement on these \nsystems precludes more than a slight chance at this point.",
